%N A400682 Number of terms in n-fold commas sequence started at 1.
%C A400682 From _Hriday Rungta_, Oct 17 2026: (Start)
%C A400682 Proof of finiteness: The sequence must terminate after finitely many terms for every n >= 1. To cross from D to D+1 digits, S must reach [10^D - 99*n, 10^D), where the leading digit of S is 9. A step T = S + n*(10*(S mod 10) + y) requires y = 9 if T < 10^D (giving S < 10^D - 10*n*(S mod 10) - 9*n), but requires y = 1 if T >= 10^D (giving S >= 10^D - 10*n*(S mod 10) - n). The intermediate open interval [10^D - 10*n*(S mod 10) - 9*n, 10^D - 10*n*(S mod 10) - n) of width 8*n constitutes an inescapable terminal trap where no digit y in {1..9} can produce a matching leading digit, forcing immediate termination.
%C A400682 Inside any constant leading-digit interval [d*10^m, (d+1)*10^m - 100*n), the leading digit of T is forced to be y = d, so (S mod 10) enters a periodic cycle of length <= 10. Fast-forwarding across each interval in O(1) time computes trajectories of length > 10^24 in milliseconds. (End)
%e A400682 Lifetimes of the n-fold commas sequences:
